    {
      "id": "c4d93113-1977-4f20-9452-231da0aaa716",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -64,
        -64
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 540,
        "height": 1016,
        "content": "## AI Daily Schedule Automation\n\n### How it works\n\n1. Every midnight, the workflow clears and resets today's schedule using a template from Google Sheets.\n2. Hourly, it gathers today's schedule, processed meeting logs, and calendar events, then merges them for AI analysis.\n3. The AI detects scheduling conflicts and attempts to rearrange the schedule accordingly.\n4. Valid AI output updates the schedule and logs processed meetings; invalid output triggers an alert email.\n5. Every 15 minutes, the workflow checks upcoming events and decides whether to call or email you based on timing.\n6. Calls go out via Twilio and emails via the Gmail node.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n- [ ] Set up Google Sheets credentials with access to the schedule, template, and logs sheets.\n- [ ] Set up Google Calendar credentials to fetch calendar events.\n- [ ] Configure the Groq (LangChain) AI model credentials.\n- [ ] Configure Gmail credentials for sending emails.\n- [ ] Configure the HTTP Request node with your Twilio API credentials.\n- [ ] Replace every placeholder value (text wrapped in square brackets) with your own details:\n    - [ReceiverEmail] in both Gmail nodes: Send Email Notification and Send AI Output Error Email\n    - [YourGoogleCalendar] in Fetch Today's Calendar Events\n    - [AccountSID], [SenderNumber], [ReceiverNumber] in Post Call Request to Twilio\n- [ ] Adjust the schedule trigger intervals if needed.\n\n### Customization\n\nChange the schedule trigger intervals, and edit the AI model prompt to fit your own conflict-detection rules."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    }
